course: dictionary
lesson: 12217
title: Conn: three meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word conn has three meanings, and the words around it tell you which one is meant. Look at the clues before you decide.
explain: A full stop after it, as in «The letter was sent to Hartford, Conn.», is a short form of Connecticut, a state in the north-east of the USA. Its postal code is CT.
explain: On a ship, «You have the conn» means you are in charge of steering and moving the ship. The officer with the conn gives the orders to the helmsman, the person who steers.
explain: Conn can also be a short name, like part of some Irish names. Conn was a legendary Irish king, so in a history book it may be a person's name.
fact: Conn. with a full stop is an old short form of Connecticut, a state in the north-east of the USA.
fact: The postal code for Connecticut is CT.
fact: The conn is the control of a ship's steering and movement.
fact: The officer «with the conn» gives orders to the helmsman.
fact: Conn is also a short name, as in Conn, a legendary Irish king.
exercise: «The address ended with Conn., USA.» What does Conn. mean here? | *Connecticut | Concord | Conference | Connor
exercise: «The captain said, "Mia, you have the conn."» What does the conn mean here? | A cold drink served on board | *Control of the ship's steering and movement | A type of sail | A list of the crew's names
exercise: In a history book, «Conn» is the name of a legendary Irish king. What kind of word is Conn here? | A place on a map | A type of ship | *A name | A sea creature
test: «The map showed Hartford, Conn., in the north-east of the USA.» What is Conn.? | A long river in Ireland | *A state in the north-east of the USA | A ship's engine room | A type of cake
test: «The helmsman waited for orders from the officer with the conn.» Who gives the orders? | The seagulls | The passengers | *The officer with the conn | The ship's cook
exam: In a sea story a sailor shouts, «I have the conn!» What is the sailor saying? | *I am in charge of steering the ship | I am hungry and want my dinner | I am lost at sea | I am going home to my family
